Zero Media independent review and verification
2026-11-20

Scope: original Zero ZMEDIA01 codec, native flash coordinator and Linux/Windows
device backends. These are test results, not a claim of physical USB validation.

Independent codec checks
Linux: 166 passing checks. Windows executable under Wine: 162 passing checks.
Five independently constructed containers matched every header and payload
byte, including their SHA-256 digest. Extracted payloads matched exactly.
Malformed magic, version, header length, sector size, payload length, flags,
digest, reserved bytes, payload data, truncation and appended bytes were refused.
Malformed extracts created no output. Existing files, input/output aliases,
directories and invalid arguments were refused. Linux also refused final
symlinks and FIFOs without modifying their targets or blocking.

The shared SHA-256 memory worker passed 145 independent Linux comparisons,
including empty input, abc, one million a bytes, every length 0 through129,
and boundaries around256,512,4096 and65536 bytes. The independent reference
implementation and orchestration are test infrastructure outside this project.

Native flash command checks
Final reviewed native coordinator: 63 passing Linux checks and61 passing
Windows/Wine checks. Valid containers produced byte-exact private regular
files, with full readback and unchanged source files. Corrupted containers
were refused before target discovery. Existing files and input aliases were
preserved. Malformed physical-device paths and missing/extra arguments failed.
Linux-specific symlink/FIFO output rejection accounts for the count difference.

Exact device-transfer implementation
media/host-io-check.zero is an original Zero test application. It creates a new
private regular file and injects its own handle into the otherwise unchanged
host I/O implementation. Production device discovery cannot use that route.
Both Linux and Windows/Wine passed18 transfer cases plus error-preserving
cleanup: actual positioned write/read, flush, unconfirmed write refusal,
zero/unaligned/oversized transfers, capacity bounds, unsigned offset overflow,
invalid handle/op/platform, partial EOF, read-only handle write failure and
preservation of a prior error. Independent comparison confirmed the expected
512-byte write and all3584 untouched guard bytes.

Coordinator control-flow checks
32 checks passed on each of Linux and Windows/Wine using the exact production
coordinator, transfer implementation, identity comparator and cleanup. Only
device discovery was replaced with an explicitly supplied private regular
file. Fault injection was confined to the separate external test executable.
Eight capacities exercised exact-image disks, one through32 remaining sectors,
33 remaining sectors, and larger tails. Image bytes, bounded zeroing of up to
33 old backup-GPT sectors and preservation of intervening bytes matched exactly.
Eight invalid confirmation forms left the target unchanged. LF and CRLF were
accepted only with the exact confirmation and target. Too-small targets and
changed device identity failed before writing. Injected flush/readback failures
were reported as failures. Read-only comparison never changed the target.

Review corrections incorporated
The shared worker stack was raised to the compiler-required81920 bytes.
Codec error cleanup retains its uniquely created partial output: it no longer
unlinks a path after closing it, avoiding deletion of a substituted file.
Native tail clearing now handles fewer than33 free sectors without overlapping
the image. Unknown backend platforms fail closed. Windows writable physical
handles require unshared access in addition to member-volume locks.

Limits
No physical USB device was written. Wine exercises the Windows executable and
regular-file Win32 transfers, not a real Windows USB storage stack. Device
discovery is deliberately restrictive; an unclassifiable Windows volume can
cause refusal. Firmware utility and boot-matrix results are reported separately.
Successful verification establishes byte agreement at readback time; it does
not establish image authenticity or power-loss durability.

Original user data remained unchanged, confirmed with the own Zero SHA tool:
